        "notes": "Running\nLet pupils answer independently, then review their answers as a class. Ask them to explain their reason for the final answer.\n\nAnswers\n1. St Paul’s Cathedral.\n2. People carried water in buckets to fight the fire.\n3. A gap left more space between buildings, making it harder for flames to reach the next house.\n\nMisconceptions\nPupils may name homes rather than St Paul’s Cathedral, or say that water was thrown from a river rather than carried in buckets. They may also think gaps helped flames spread; explain that gaps made it harder for flames to reach another house.\n\nBackground\nThese questions check recall of the destroyed landmark and the ways people tried to control the fire.",
        "question": {
          "type": "open-response",
          "modelAnswer": "1. St Paul’s Cathedral\n2. They carried water in buckets.\n3. The flames had a gap to cross instead of another close house to burn."
        },
