        "notes": "Running\nUse the bar model to show that the fifth-sized parts continue past one whole. Ask pupils how many fifths make one whole and how many fifths remain.\n\nMisconceptions\nPupils may think a fraction cannot be greater than one whole. Explain that six fifths means six fifth-sized parts: five make one whole, and the sixth extends beyond it.\n\nBackground\nAn improper fraction has a numerator greater than its denominator and represents more than one whole.",
